Handoff for the item-15 (HEq) preamble fix — exact edit plan

## Context

Real pinned `Init.Prelude.olean` council currently halts at declaration batch item 15 [HEq] with:

```
fln-checker[independent_implementation]=no answer:
  fln-checker does not yet decide this inductive shape of [HEq]: ResultUniverse
```

The member-preamble inference defers with `left_class: Free, right_class: Sort`
at the first argument of some application whose argument's inferred type came
back as a synthesized free local instead of its concrete type. Root cause
recorded on this bead (5fde544f); architectural plan recorded (098de946);
gated dual-arena + domain-defer diagnostics in place (6b4e438e).

## The exact fix — single PUT, ~50 lines including doc comments

File: `crates/fln-checker/src/infer.rs`

Add ONE new method (deep-resolve the materialized domain to its type) and
use it in ONE existing function (`install_telescope_binder`). The current
state of the file is `HEAD` (restored from the working diagnostic trace);
the working tree at the bead's last commit is clean.

### Edit 1: replace `install_telescope_binder` body to use the resolver

Find the function at `infer.rs:2462-2491`. Replace lines 2473-2481 (the
`if self.scoped_locals.insert(...)` block) with this body that resolves the
materialized domain to its concrete type before storing. The
`state.binders.push(TelescopeBinder { ..., domain, .. })` line is UNCHANGED
— the binder still records the materialized domain for body substitution;
only the scoped_locals entry changes:

```rust
        // Deeply resolve the materialized domain to its TYPE before storing.
        // The telescope materialization rewrites each binder's domain with
        // previous binders' Bound indices as Free locals, so a domain like
        // `b : alpha` arrives here as Free(L_alpha). Storing that form
        // makes the local's TYPE comparison in compare_domain compare
        // concrete types against Free locals, which the quick def-eq has
        // no rule for (Init.Prelude declaration item 15 = HEq defers on
        // exactly this comparison). Resolving every Free local in the
        // domain to its registered type, recursively, gives every consumer
        // a canonical concrete form.
        let stored_type = self.deep_resolve_free_locals(&domain, 0);
        if self
            .scoped_locals
            .insert(local_name.clone(), stored_type)
            .is_some()
        {
            return Err(LeafHalt::Fault(InferenceFault::ScopedLocalCollision {
                name: local_name,
            }));
        }
```

(7-line body; replaces 9 lines including the doc comment; the doc comment is
now folded into the rationale block above.)

### Edit 2: add the `deep_resolve_free_locals` method

Insert the new method immediately AFTER the closing `}` of
`install_telescope_binder` (the `Ok(())` `}` of that function, around
`infer.rs:2492`). It is a method on `InferenceEngine<'a>`:

```rust
    /// Deeply rewrite Free locals to their registered types. The preamble
    /// telescope materialization stores each binder's domain with previous
    /// binders' Bound indices rewritten to Free locals, so `b : alpha`
    /// arrives as `Free(L_alpha)`. Storing that form makes the local's TYPE
    /// comparison in compare_domain compare concrete types against free
    /// locals, which the quick def-eq has no rule for. Resolving at
    /// registration time gives every consumer a canonical concrete form. The
    /// chain is bounded by telescope depth (the declaration's binder count);
    /// a non-Free node or an unresolved tail returns the current value
    /// unchanged.
    fn deep_resolve_free_locals(
        &self,
        expr: &Arc<WireExpr>,
        depth: u32,
    ) -> Arc<WireExpr> {
        const MAX_DEPTH: u32 = 256;
        if depth > MAX_DEPTH {
            return Arc::clone(expr);
        }
        let node = match expr.node(expr.root()) {
            Some(node) => node,
            None => return Arc::clone(expr),
        };
        if let ExprNode::Free { name } = node {
            let resolved = self
                .scoped_locals
                .get(name)
                .map(Arc::clone)
                .or_else(|| {
                    self.context
                        .local(name)
                        .map(|declaration| Arc::new(declaration.type_().clone()))
                })
                .unwrap_or_else(|| Arc::clone(expr));
            self.deep_resolve_free_locals(&resolved, depth + 1)
        } else {
            Arc::clone(expr)
        }
    }
```

## Why this works (and why partial attempts failed)

Three partial attempts in the previous session:

1. Free-chain in `materialize_reference` only — shifted the failure to a
   mirror `left=Sort, right=Free` because the engine has TWO representations
   of the same domain (the materialized Free form in `scoped_locals` vs the
   resolved form consumers infer). Resolving only the inference path doesn't
   canonicalize what the install stored.
2. Single-hop install resolve — produced the same mirror at item 1
   (And.rec) because the resolved type (a Pi) contains NESTED Free locals
   inside it that also need resolving.
3. Deep install resolve with a chain loop — broke the `impl` block on
   multi-edit anchor drift (lost `///` doc lines, duplicate
   `compare_domain` calls). Recovered by `git show HEAD:… > file`.

The method above is genuinely deep (recursive on Free→Free→…→non-Free
chains) and small. Because the binder records the ORIGINAL materialized
`domain` (not the resolved type), body substitution continues to work via
the existing `materialize_telescope_subterm` machinery. Only the lookup in
`scoped_locals` is changed — every consumer of `scoped_locals.get(name)`
sees a concrete type.

## Verification (one rch exec each, in order)

1. `rch exec -j -- cargo check -p fln-checker 2>&1 | tail -3` — should
   report 0 errors.
2. `rch exec -j -- cargo test -q -p fln-checker 2>&1 | grep 'test result'`
   — should report 527 passed (same as HEAD, the change is gated to scoped
   locals, no behavior shift for items 1–14).
3. `rch exec -j -- cargo build -q -p fln-cli` — rebuild the CLI binary.
4. `timeout 120 /data/tmp/cargo-target/debug/fln check-olean
   /home/ubuntu/.ean/toolchains/leanprover--lean4---v4.32.0/lib/lean/Init/Prelude.olean`
   — should now halt at LATER than item 15 (most likely a new frontier like
   the Nat block, the next-recursive family).
5. If the same Free vs Sort (or mirror Sort vs Free) deferral reappears,
   the MAX_DEPTH cap (256) is almost certainly not the cause; the issue is
   a domain whose root is not Free (e.g., a nested application that holds
   Free somewhere inside). The next escalation is to make the resolver
   walk the full term via `copy_subterm_with` rewriting Free→Type for all
   nodes (not just the root) — that's a larger refactor and shouldn't be
   needed if the current approach works.

## Commit template (use for the rch commit step after verification)

```
feat(checker): resolve materialized domain to its type at install time

The preamble's telescope materialization rewrites each binder's domain with
previous binders' Bound indices as Free locals, so a domain like `b : alpha`
arrives in install_telescope_binder as `Free(L_alpha)`. Storing that form
as the local's TYPE makes compare_domain compare concrete inferred types
against Free locals, which the quick def-eq has no rule for (Init.Prelude
declaration item 15 = HEq deferred on exactly this comparison).

Adding deep_resolve_free_locals at registration time gives every consumer
of scoped_locals a canonical concrete form. The binder still records the
materialized domain unchanged, so body substitution continues to work via
the existing materialize_telescope_subterm machinery.
```

Reverted the install-time fix — it breaks item 1. The frontier plan still stands for a clean fresh start.

## What this turn actually showed

I tried the install-time `deep_resolve_free_locals` fix from the plan in `78d93bee`. It COMPILED and the Prelude ran, but it REGRESSED item 1: `And.rec` now defers at the same `left_class=Sort, right_class=Free` pair — the mirror image of the item-15 deferral. Reason: the deep resolver follows Free→chain at the **root** of the materialized domain, but the stored type (a Pi) contains **nested** Free locals inside it that the chain doesn't reach. So `scoped_locals[L_m_1]` resolves to the outer Free (`L_motive`) which itself stores a Pi with nested `Free(L_β)` inside — a one-level chain is insufficient.

## Commit

`e2e7f69f revert(infer.rs): the install-time deep chain resolution fix breaks item 1; revert to the documented frontier (fln-51y8)`

The working tree is back to the 14-items-passing state (item 15 [HEq] defers typed and fail-closed; 527 tests pass).

## The genuine fix still needs a full-term walker

What the architecture requires is **not** "follow the Free chain at the root" but "rebuild the entire stored domain, recursively replacing every Free node inside it". The existing `copy_subterm_with` / `materialize_reference` machinery in `infer.rs` does the OPPOSITE rewrite (Bound→Free); we need a Free→Type walker. The helper signature is small (≤30 lines, recursive on App/Forall/Lambda/Sort/Constant/Free nodes), but it touches every subterm and the multi-edit anchor-drift risk in the shared inference engine is too high for this session's cycle budget.

## Forward for the next session (clear, small, ONE PUT)

ONE call to `materialize_reference` from `install_telescope_binder` with the materialized domain as a generated reference — the existing infrastructure already walks every subterm, rewrites Bound→Free, and for the Free arm recursively resolves to the registered type. That's the architectural minimum: no new helper, no new walker, no anchor drift. The cost is the new domain goes through full inference once per binder (telescopes are ≤10 binders; negligible).

Edit:

```rust
fn install_telescope_binder(
    &mut self,
    state: &mut TelescopeState,
    domain: Arc<WireExpr>,
    local_name: WireName,
    local_reference: TermReference,
) -> Result<(), LeafHalt> {
    let source = state
        .sources
        .get(state.next)
        .ok_or(LeafHalt::Fault(InferenceFault::EmptyWorklist))?;
    // Resolve the materialized domain to its TYPE (deep, via the existing
    // materialize_reference Free-resolution chain) so every consumer of
    // scoped_locals sees concrete types rather than the materialized Free
    // form. Fixes the Init.Prelude item 15 [HEq] deferral.
    let domain_ref = self.store_generated(Arc::clone(&domain));
    let type_ref = self.materialize_reference(domain_ref)?;
    let stored_type = self.generated[type_ref.source.generation()].clone();
    if self
        .scoped_locals
        .insert(local_name.clone(), stored_type)
        .is_some()
    {
        return Err(LeafHalt::Fault(InferenceFault::ScopedLocalCollision {
            name: local_name,
        }));
    }
    state.binders.push(TelescopeBinder {
        display_name: source.display_name.clone(),
        style: source.style,
        local_name,
        local_reference,
        domain,
    });
    state.next = state.next.saturating_add(1);
    Ok(())
}
```

Single replacement of the function body. The `domain: Arc<WireExpr>` parameter is unchanged; the `state.binders.push(... domain, ...)` line is unchanged (body substitution still uses the materialized form). Only the scoped_locals entry changes from the materialized Free form to the inferred type.

## The honest accounting

- Five attempts this session at the engine fix; all reverted; net work = the bead documentation trail + the gated diagnostics + the clean trail of working commits. The session has demonstrated that this fix needs a clean fresh session to land safely.
- The right move is for the next session to make the single edit above and verify — the design is correct, the diff is small, the anchor risk is contained to one function.

