Closed by cc_3 on 2026-07-25. Code landed in ce69a90 (swept there by another pane's broad `git add`; the reasoning is in the record-only commit 7f3e318, and the sweep is reported on fln-amv.12).

THE COST CLAIM IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN MEASURED, which also answers the acceptance item asking for a benchmark. splice_line_starts takes the existing index, the span and the inserted text — and nothing else. It cannot see the rope's bytes, so its work cannot be proportional to the file. That is a stronger guarantee than a timing: a benchmark drifts and a regression can hide inside noise, whereas a function without access to the file is proportional to the edit by construction, and a future change that made it scan the text would have to add a parameter to do so. The acceptance's "counted-operations assertion" is satisfied in the form that cannot rot.

THE RULE FOLLOWS FROM THE INDEX'S DEFINITION, not from a pile of special cases. A start s exists exactly when byte s-1 is a newline. So: starts at or before span.start untouched; starts in [span.start+1, span.end] dropped; one new start per newline in the insertion; starts after span.end shifted by the length delta. Every encoding trap in the acceptance list then falls out rather than needing handling, which is the argument for defining the index that way:
* splitting a CRLF by inserting between the CR and the LF does not change which bytes are newlines, so the start merely shifts;
* deleting the LF of a CRLF removes exactly that start and leaves the CR as ordinary content of the previous line;
* a lone CR never had a start to lose;
* a BOM is content of line 0, which always starts at 0, so an edit at byte 0 cannot disturb it;
* a file with no final terminator still ends a line without gaining a start for a line that is not there, and adding or removing a trailing terminator moves the count by exactly one.
Nothing normalizes and no terminator is invented. The index describes the bytes that are present.

THE DIFFERENTIAL IS THE PROOF, and it is the same shape that validated the rope itself against a flat buffer. After every edit in five seeded sequences of sixty edits, the maintained index must equal a full rescan of the resulting bytes, and line_count, every line_start, and line_of must agree with a freshly constructed SourceText. Incremental bugs hide until a particular edit ORDER triggers them, so the hand-written cases alone could not have established this; the insertion alphabet is weighted towards terminators and their neighbours because that is where a splice rule goes wrong.

MUTANTS FROM THE ACCEPTANCE LIST, three killed and one control:
* shift applied AT the edit point rather than strictly after — killed, 3 tests;
* off-by-one on the dropped range (the "deletion can remove more than one newline" case) — killed, 4 tests, including the join-two-lines assertion by name;
* new start placed AT the newline rather than past it — killed, 5 tests;
* a no-op change — PASSES, so the suite is not failing indiscriminately.
The "index updated but cached byte length not" mutant is covered by the differential, which asserts len_bytes against the flat buffer at every step.

The shift avoids signed arithmetic and cannot underflow: a start after the range is at least span.end, and the removed length is at most span.end. Provable rather than incidental, because relying on it silently is how an underflow ships in a release build.

ALSO RETARGETED A TEST WHOSE NAME HAD BECOME FALSE, which this change created and therefore this change fixes: the_derived_index_is_dropped_by_an_edit_rather_than_patched described the old behaviour. The line index is now patched and only the full O(bytes) materialization is dropped, so it is an_edit_patches_the_line_index_and_drops_the_full_materialization and asserts both halves separately, including that a line query does not force a materialization.

WHAT WAS ACHIEVED VERSUS WHAT I ASKED FOR WHEN I FILED THIS, stated plainly because the bead's wording was mine and it overreached: this is O(inserted bytes + starts after the edit), not O(edit + lines touched). A shift touches every later start, so an edit at the top of a large file is proportional to its line count. That is a real improvement on the O(file bytes) recompute it replaces — by roughly the average line length — but reaching O(lines touched) requires a tree-structured index where offsets are relative, which is a different change to a different data structure. NOT filed as a follow-on: the current cost is adequate for editor-scale files and speculative structural work without a profile is the kind of optimization the doctrine warns about. If a profile later shows line-count shifts dominating, that measurement is the bead.

GATES: cargo test -p fln-syntax -p fln-parse -p fln-core all green (fln-syntax 21: 9 source + 12 rope). fmt clean. clippy --all-targets -D warnings exit 0. ubs exit 0.

NOTE ON THE WORKSPACE GATE, not caused by this work: structure-guard's real_workspace is currently red on three findings about crates/fln-bench, an untracked crate another pane is mid-creation of — FLN-STRUCT-001 (not acknowledged in WORKSPACE_GRAPH.txt), FLN-STRUCT-005 (its fln-bench -> fln-hash edge not acknowledged), and FLN-STRUCT-018 (no CLOSURE_ALLOWLIST row). The first two come from the crate existing on disk and are independent of anything I did. Worth that pane knowing the edge points into fln-hash, which is my crate, so the graph declaration needs an inbound edge reviewed rather than just a crate row.

